{
  "id": "job-timeout-leaked-interval",
  "title": "Test job hits its 15-minute timeout because a module-level setInterval keeps Jest alive",
  "description": "PR #154 adds src/metrics/batcher.ts (MetricsBatcher starts a setInterval in its constructor, never unref'd) and a module-level instance in src/metrics/index.ts, imported by the order handlers. Every test passes (18 suites, 214 tests in ~42 s), but the interval keeps the Node event loop alive, Jest prints 'Jest did not exit one second after the test run has completed' and hangs until the job's timeout-minutes: 15 cancels it. The log's loudest lines are the job-timeout annotation (the 'Job exceeded its time limit' signature, categorised infrastructure, whose hint suggests raising timeout-minutes) and 'The operation was canceled'. On main the same job passes on all of the last 10 runs. Correct answer: code, flaky=false: the PR's timer keeps the process alive; unref() it (or create the batcher lazily / stop it in test teardown). Raising the timeout or rerunning would hang the same way.",
